---

<Guide
  title="Subscriptions and accounts"
  description="Sign in to the plans you already pay for, and add more than one. When a lane uses up one plan’s allowance, move it to another and keep going."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="plans">
    <h2>Which plans work</h2>
    <p>
      You don’t need Claude Code to use Phosphor. Any provider pi supports works, including API keys
      and your own endpoints. These are the subscription plans you can sign in with:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>ChatGPT Plus or Pro:</strong> pi’s built-in Codex sign-in, under Settings → Accounts.
        You don’t need the Codex CLI.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Claude Pro or Max:</strong> the official Claude Code CLI plus the <code
          >@saccolabs/pi-claude-cli</code
        > provider. Its logins live under Settings → Extensions → Claude Code, and you can add several.
      </li><li>
        <strong>GitHub Copilot:</strong> Settings → Accounts. Copilot Enterprise Server uses pi’s terminal
        login.
      </li><li><strong>Kimi For Coding:</strong> Settings → Accounts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Settings → Accounts also has a direct Anthropic sign-in, but it bills extra usage rather than
      your plan’s allowance. To spend a Claude Pro or Max plan, use the Claude Code provider. API
      keys and custom endpoints are set up in pi: see <a href={piDoc('providers')}>providers</a> and <a
        href={piDoc('models')}>custom models</a
      > in pi’s docs.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="recommended">
    <h2>The setup we recommend</h2>
    <p>
      Every plan has a limit: a 5-hour window, a weekly cap, or a monthly allowance, depending on
      the plan. With one plan, hitting it stops your work. With several, a lane moves to the next
      one and keeps going.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Two or more Claude Max accounts</strong> through Claude Code. Each login is kept separately,
        and routing can spread new sessions across them.
      </li><li>
        <strong>ChatGPT through Codex</strong>, for a second model family on its own allowance.
      </li><li><strong>Kimi For Coding or Copilot</strong>, if you have them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Only add accounts you’re allowed to use. This spreads your work across plans you already have.
      It doesn’t lift any provider’s limits or change their terms.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="pi-accounts">
    <h2>Sign in to ChatGPT, Copilot, and Kimi</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open Settings with Cmd+, on macOS or Ctrl+, on Windows/Linux, then select <strong
          >Accounts</strong
        >. Each provider pi can sign into has a row that says whether it’s ready.
      </li><li>
        Choose <strong>Sign in</strong> and finish in your browser. A ChatGPT row then shows which account
        is signed in.
      </li><li>
        pi keeps one sign-in per provider. <strong>Sign in again</strong> switches that provider to another
        account.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      If sign-in from Settings doesn’t finish, run <code>pi</code> in a terminal and use <code
        >/login</code
      >. These are pi’s own credentials. Claude Code logins are kept separately, below.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="prerequisites">
    <h2>Claude: before you begin</h2>
    <p>
      Finish <a href="/guides/#install">installing Phosphor and pi</a> first. You also need the official
      Claude Code CLI and a Claude plan that covers how you plan to use it. This guide covers Pro and
      Max. Anthropic’s terms, limits, and extra-usage charges still apply.
    </p>
    <p>
      Install Claude Code with its <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup"
        >official setup instructions</a
      > and check that <code>claude --version</code> works. Phosphor needs <code
        >pi-claude-cli 0.10.0</code
      > or newer and Claude Code <code>2.1.263</code> or newer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The provider drives the official CLI for you. Don’t pull a token out of your Claude
      subscription or paste one into pi’s API key settings.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="install">
    <h2>Claude: install the provider</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open Settings with Cmd+, on macOS or Ctrl+, on Windows/Linux.</li><li>
        Select <strong>Extensions</strong>. Under Recommended, find <strong
          >Claude Code provider</strong
        > and choose <strong>Add</strong>. If the card says it needs the Claude CLI on your PATH,
        install the CLI and make it visible to Phosphor before testing the provider.
      </li><li>
        Wait for the install job to finish. A <strong>Claude Code</strong> tab then shows up under Extensions.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>Or from a terminal:</p>
    <Command
      cmd="pi install npm:@saccolabs/pi-claude-cli"
      label="Install the Claude Code provider"
    />
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-extensions')}
      alt="Extensions settings showing the recommended package catalog in a demo configuration."
      caption="Settings → Extensions: find the Claude Code provider"
      provenance="Settings demo · example data"
    />
    <p>
      Install globally the first time. A project install only applies to that workspace, and
      different global and project versions get confusing fast. After installing or updating, let
      running work finish and start a new session.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="sign-in">
    <h2>Claude: add your first account</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>Settings → Extensions → Claude Code</strong>.</li><li>
        Check the Health rows: the extension must be installed and the <code>claude</code> binary found.
      </li><li>
        If your existing Claude CLI login shows up, make sure it’s the account you want. If not,
        choose <strong>Add account</strong>.
      </li><li>
        Finish signing in to Claude in your browser. If Phosphor asks for a code, paste the one
        Claude shows you into <strong>Paste code</strong> and choose Continue. Don’t share that code.
      </li><li>
        Check that the account row appears. Use <strong>Sign in again</strong> to renew an expired login.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-claude')}
      alt="Claude Code settings showing Health and two fictional accounts labeled Account A and Account B, with addresses masked."
      caption="Health and Accounts live in the Claude Code tab"
      provenance="Settings demo · masked examples"
    />
    <p>
      <strong>Two places, two kinds of account:</strong> Settings → Accounts holds pi’s provider sign-ins.
      The Claude Code tab holds the Claude CLI logins this package uses.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="verify">
    <h2>Claude: test the provider, then select it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Scroll to <strong>Prove it end to end</strong> and choose <strong>Test provider</strong>.
      </li><li>
        Check the job output. A working round trip prints <code>phosphor-provider-ok</code> and a confirmation.
        This sends a small real prompt, so it uses a little plan quota.
      </li><li>
        Start a new session. In the composer’s model menu, choose a Claude model under <code
          >pi-claude-cli</code
        >.
      </li><li>
        Send a small prompt. Open the context meter to check which Claude account the session is
        using.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Leave the context window setting at its default for now. A bigger window means more history
      before compaction, but each request can re-read more context. It doesn’t raise your plan’s
      limits.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="multiple-accounts">
    <h2>Claude: add another authorized account</h2>
    <p>
      In the same tab, choose <strong>Add account</strong> again. Each login is stored separately, so
      adding one doesn’t replace another.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the browser signs you straight back into the first account, sign out at claude.ai and try
      again. Signing in with an address that’s already listed just renews that row.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only add accounts you’re allowed to use. Routing spreads your sessions across them. It doesn’t
      lift provider limits or change anyone’s terms. Choose <strong>Refresh usage</strong> to update the
      numbers routing uses for its next pick.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="routing">
    <h2>Claude: choose how new sessions are routed</h2>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-routing')}
      alt="The Route new sessions controls with One account, In order top to bottom, and Round robin options; all accounts are fictional."
      caption="Routing picks the account for new sessions only"
      provenance="Settings demo · masked examples"
    />
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One account:</strong> every new session uses the account you pick.</li><li>
        <strong>In order, top to bottom:</strong> new sessions use the first eligible account. Reorder
        with the arrows. Accounts that are unavailable, out of quota, or spending overage credits can
        be skipped.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Round robin:</strong> new sessions take turns across eligible accounts.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Usage numbers can be cached. If they’re missing or stale, don’t assume the account has room.
      When it matters, check usage and billing with the provider.
    </p>
    <aside class="notice">
      <strong>Routing only picks for new sessions</strong><p>
        A running session keeps the account it started with. Open an account row to see its usage
        and live sessions, and move one to another account from there or from the context meter. <a
          href="#pivot">When a plan runs out</a
        > covers what a move costs.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section id="pivot">
    <h2>When a plan runs out</h2>
    <p>
      Open the context meter in the composer to see how much of your Claude or ChatGPT plan is used,
      per window, with reset times. When a Claude account gets close to its limit, a banner also
      shows above the composer.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Move to another Claude account:</strong> in the context meter, choose <strong
          >Switch account</strong
        >. The lane restarts on that account from its saved history.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Move to another model:</strong> pick one from the composer’s model menu, from any provider
        you’re signed into. The conversation carries over.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Either way, the next turn re-reads the whole conversation on the new plan, so it costs
      context. Let the current turn finish first. With In order or Round robin routing, new sessions
      can also skip a Claude account that’s at its limit or spending overage credits.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="troubleshooting">
    <h2>Troubleshooting and updates</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Claude Code tab missing:</strong> check the provider is installed in the right scope,
        then reopen Settings.
      </li><li>
        <strong>CLI not found:</strong> check the path under Health. Apps launched from the desktop don’t
        always get the Node or shell setup your terminal has.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Package or CLI is old:</strong> each has its own Update control. From a terminal: <code
          >pi update npm:@saccolabs/pi-claude-cli</code
        > and <code>claude update</code>. Updating one doesn’t update the other.
      </li><li>
        <strong>A new model is missing:</strong> the model list comes from pi, not the Claude CLI, so
        update pi too.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Old session reports a context-policy change:</strong> choose <strong
          >Rebuild the Claude session</strong
        >. The next turn reloads saved history, which costs one full-context cache write.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Claude can’t see your existing MCP servers:</strong> that’s on purpose. Phosphor uses
        pi’s built-in MCP, not Claude’s own MCP config. <a href="/guides/connectors/"
          >Add those services in MCP Connectors</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('cli-providers.md')}>Provider architecture and version requirements</a> · <a
        href={doc('settings.md#accounts')}>Account reference</a
      > · <a href="https://github.com/agustinsacco/pi-claude-cli">Provider source and releases</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
